WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.060
DBSCAN groups points by density.

2
00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.689
A point with at least min-samples

3
00:00:06.758 --> 00:00:09.796
points within distance eps, itself included

4
00:00:09.796 --> 00:00:10.970
is a core point.

5
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.395
Core points within eps of each

6
00:00:13.464 --> 00:00:15.400
other link into one cluster.

7
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.871
A non-core point next to a core

8
00:00:17.939 --> 00:00:20.950
point is a border point; the rest are noise.

9
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.288
On our one hundred fifty points

10
00:00:23.288 --> 00:00:24.966
that gives three clusters

11
00:00:24.966 --> 00:00:27.700
with five border points and fourteen noise.

12
00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.947
The task checks a result against the plain

13
00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:33.677
version with the adjusted Rand index, ARI

14
00:00:33.677 --> 00:00:36.280
which ignores how clusters are numbered.

15
00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:39.087
But swap the noise with a whole cluster

16
00:00:39.087 --> 00:00:40.439
and ARI is still one

17
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.097
although forty-two percent

18
00:00:42.159 --> 00:00:43.510
of the points changed.

19
00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.900
So the task also counts noise mismatches.

20
00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.400
Five of sixteen passed.

21
00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:52.812
Gemini 4 Argon in Pi was the fastest

22
00:00:52.812 --> 00:00:54.550
and failed on noise.

23
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.984
Claude Opus 5.5 took two point

24
00:00:57.053 --> 00:00:58.720
three five milliseconds

25
00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.220
against the reference's twenty-one.

26
00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:03.169
It skipped tile pairs that

27
00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:04.337
lie too far apart

28
00:01:04.337 --> 00:01:06.858
settled the clear pairs on tensor cores

29
00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.642
and rechecked only the pairs

30
00:01:08.703 --> 00:01:09.810
near the boundary.

31
00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.320
Its noise matched exactly.

32
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.909
IVF-PQ is a standard index for approximate

33
00:01:15.981 --> 00:01:17.700
nearest-neighbor search.

34
00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:20.417
IVF splits the vectors into lists

35
00:01:20.489 --> 00:01:22.210
by their nearest center.

36
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.372
PQ compresses each vector's offset from

37
00:01:25.445 --> 00:01:27.680
that center into m short codes.

38
00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:30.973
A query probes only its few nearest lists

39
00:01:30.973 --> 00:01:33.566
and builds one small table per list

40
00:01:33.636 --> 00:01:35.528
so each stored vector costs

41
00:01:35.598 --> 00:01:37.070
just m table lookups.

42
00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:38.909
Pause and predict.

43
00:01:38.979 --> 00:01:40.797
Our index has two thousand

44
00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:42.616
vectors in sixteen lists.

45
00:01:42.686 --> 00:01:45.413
Probing four lists instead of one scans

46
00:01:45.483 --> 00:01:47.651
about four times as many codes.

47
00:01:47.651 --> 00:01:49.330
What happens to recall?

48
00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:55.839
It rises from zero point eight five to one.

49
00:01:55.839 --> 00:01:57.009
Past four lists

50
00:01:57.009 --> 00:01:59.280
more scanning buys nothing here.

51
00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.480
On the real task, eight of sixteen passed.

52
00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:05.711
Claude Opus 5.5 took one point

53
00:02:05.774 --> 00:02:07.341
seven three milliseconds

54
00:02:07.404 --> 00:02:08.846
against the reference's

55
00:02:08.909 --> 00:02:10.288
one hundred fifty-one

56
00:02:10.351 --> 00:02:12.232
by decoding each list once for

57
00:02:12.295 --> 00:02:13.988
every query that probes it

58
00:02:13.988 --> 00:02:15.430
as one matrix product.

59
00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.153
It scored eighty point five against

60
00:02:18.221 --> 00:02:19.780
seventy-five point six.

61
00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:21.415
That gap looks small

62
00:02:21.415 --> 00:02:23.250
but the score is logarithmic

63
00:02:23.311 --> 00:02:25.758
it means about ten times the reference's

64
00:02:25.819 --> 00:02:27.410
speed-up over the starter.

65
00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:30.904
That ends the lecture on machine-learning systems

66
00:02:30.904 --> 00:02:32.070
with three lessons.

67
00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.460
Estimate what you skip, and rank by error.

68
00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.372
Every workload, every metric

69
00:02:38.372 --> 00:02:41.730
no model passed SVG-EAR or Continuum.

70
00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:45.321
And bound, then be exact: Opus's k-NN

71
00:02:45.321 --> 00:02:48.648
DBSCAN and IVF-PQ passes compute fast

72
00:02:48.648 --> 00:02:51.292
prove how far off they can be

73
00:02:51.292 --> 00:02:54.960
and redo only what lies near the boundary.

74
00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:57.024
Next time: Lecture 2

75
00:02:57.024 --> 00:02:59.310
high-performance computing.
